WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.651
Premises and equipment must be suitable
for their intended pharmaceutical

2
00:00:03.651 --> 00:00:09.080
manufacturing operations. Good Manufacturing
Practice expects facility design,

3
00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:14.640
construction, maintenance and use to
minimize errors and contamination risks.

4
00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:19.080
Controls depend on the product,
process and manufacturing environment.

5
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.420
This lesson introduces the principles
rather than prescribing one universal room

6
00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:30.060
design or classification. Facility layout
should support logical personnel and

7
00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:34.260
material movement and help
prevent contamination and mix-ups.

8
00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:38.061
Separate or appropriately controlled
flows may be necessary depending on the

9
00:00:38.061 --> 00:00:43.800
operation and risks. Material receipt,
storage, dispensing, production and

10
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.360
dispatch each require
suitable arrangements.

11
00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:51.660
The objective is controlled movement
and traceability, not simply a visually

12
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:56.760
attractive floor plan. Cleaning and
housekeeping are essential routine controls.

13
00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.900
Cleaning procedures should specify
responsibilities, methods, materials and

14
00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.351
frequencies appropriate
to the area or equipment.

15
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.100
Where necessary, cleaning effectiveness
must be demonstrated and documented.

16
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.740
Personnel should promptly report spills,
damage or abnormal conditions according

17
00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:19.640
to site procedures. A clean appearance
alone is not evidence that a cleaning

18
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:24.680
process is effective. Manufacturing
equipment should be appropriately designed,

19
00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.520
located and maintained
for its intended use.

20
00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:31.811
Product contact surfaces should be
suitable for the materials and processes

21
00:01:31.811 --> 00:01:36.060
involved. Equipment should allow
effective cleaning and, where required,

22
00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.760
sterilization. The level of control
depends on criticality and intended

23
00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:46.020
function. For example, a filling machine
and a general office printer have

24
00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:51.520
different GMP impacts. Calibration
establishes the relationship between an

25
00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:55.220
instrument indication and a
reference under specified conditions.

26
00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:59.360
Maintenance helps keep equipment
in suitable operating condition.

27
00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:04.520
Both should be planned, recorded and
assessed for impact when overdue or

28
00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:10.080
unsatisfactory. A calibration label is
a useful visual control but does not

29
00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.240
replace review of the applicable
calibration record or equipment status.

30
00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:19.720
Qualification provides documented
evidence that facilities, utilities and

31
00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.380
equipment are suitable for
their intended purpose.

32
00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:27.200
Installation qualification, operational
qualification and performance

33
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.440
qualification are commonly
described as IQ, OQ and PQ.

34
00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:37.660
Their application and scope should follow
a justified lifecycle and risk-based

35
00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:43.280
approach. Acceptance criteria must be
defined for the actual system and process;

36
00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.240
there is no universal test limit
for every item of equipment.

37
00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:52.760
Clear equipment identification and status
controls help prevent unintended use.

38
00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:58.100
Site procedures may use status labels,
electronic systems, logbooks and approved

39
00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:03.180
records to show equipment availability,
maintenance, calibration, cleaning or

40
00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:07.220
qualification state. Operators
should verify status before use.

41
00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:12.500
If information is missing or inconsistent,
the discrepancy should be escalated

42
00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:17.460
rather than resolved by assumption.
Consider a vessel needed for a scheduled

43
00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:22.860
manufacturing batch. A measuring instrument
used with the vessel has an expired

44
00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:28.200
calibration due date, and its calibration
records contain no current evidence of

45
00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:32.960
calibration. The operator should not
assume that the instrument or the planned

46
00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:38.240
operation is acceptable. Report the
finding so authorized personnel can assess

47
00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:43.740
the instrument status, its intended use,
and any potential product impact under

48
00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:48.340
site procedures. Work should proceed
only after appropriate documented

49
00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:49.100
authorization.
